1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,938
In January 2026, a gallon of regular gas cost well
under its average for Joe Biden's presidency.

2
00:00:07,967 --> 00:00:10,304
By spring, it cost well over it.

3
00:00:10,333 --> 00:00:14,733
So is gas cheaper now than under Biden?
That depends on what you mean by now.

4
00:00:15,733 --> 00:00:25,483
President Trump made the case at a cabinet meeting: "It was $4 and
$5 for a gallon of gas", he said, "and now it's probably 225".

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:31,975
By this spring, CBS News was reporting pump
prices "at their highest point since mid-2022".

6
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:42,688
Here is the record to check them against: the Energy Information Administration's
national monthly average for regular gas, in dollars not adjusted for inflation.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:54,125
From February 2021 through January 2025, it came to
$3.46 a gallon, with a peak of $4.93 in June 2022.

8
00:00:54,133 --> 00:01:02,358
In 2025 the lowest month, December, came to
$2.89, well above the president's guess.

9
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:09,529
Every month of 2025 was below the Biden-era average,
but about level with the last months of his term.

10
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:18,383
Then the weekly price jumped, from $3.02 in the
first week of March 2026 to $4.50 by mid-May.

11
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:32,838
News reports tied the jump to the war with Iran: MS NOW said gas was "up by well over a dollar since the war
started", and NBC News put it plainly: "it's the war in Iran that's largely causing fuel prices to rise".

12
00:01:32,867 --> 00:01:44,579
West Texas Intermediate crude oil jumped in the same month,
from $64.51 a barrel in February to $91.38 in March.

13
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:52,875
Those reports tie the rise to the war; the price record shows when
prices jumped, but on its own it cannot rule out other causes.

14
00:01:53,400 --> 00:02:00,813
In October, President Trump made the same kind of claim about
crude: "Oil was higher under Biden than it is under Trump".

15
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:12,571
In September, the latest month in the EIA's crude table, it averaged $97 a barrel,
higher than in every month of Biden's term except March through July 2022.

16
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:18,600
Gas, meanwhile, averaged
$4.36 a gallon in September.

17
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:31,763
Put the lines side by side and the pump price has moved with crude under both presidents: both
peaked in June 2022, both ended 2025 lower than they started it, and both jumped in March 2026.

18
00:02:31,767 --> 00:02:44,417
That fits what a guest on PBS NewsHour said, that the pump price "is set in a global market"; but
prices moving together cannot show that policy played no part, since policy can move crude itself.

19
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:58,121
So whether gas is cheaper than under Biden depends on the month: yes, against his term's average,
through February 2026; no, in every month since March, though still short of that 2022 peak.

20
00:02:59,133 --> 00:03:03,133
But gas is one price among many.
What about prices overall?

21
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:10,671
The channel Squirrelly Economics put the comparison
this way: "Prices rose about 21% over Biden's 4 years".

22
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:20,087
The Bureau of Labor Statistics' consumer price index agrees:
it rose 21.4 percent from January 2021 to January 2025.

23
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:28,125
From January 2025 to September 2026,
the latest month, it rose 6.1 percent.

24
00:03:28,133 --> 00:03:33,846
But that sets a full term against one still
under way, over a much shorter stretch.

25
00:03:34,367 --> 00:03:51,254
Over stretches as long as this one so far, at each end of Biden's term, prices rose 13.5 percent at
the start, January 2021 to September 2022, and 4.5 percent at the end, May 2023 to January 2025.

26
00:03:51,267 --> 00:04:02,454
And the count is still running: the year-on-year rate climbed from 2.4 percent
in February 2026 to 3.7 percent in September, the same months gas jumped.

27
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:16,454
So prices have risen more slowly this term than across Biden's term as a whole, though faster than in his last stretch,
and by a smaller margin than the totals suggest; and the index cannot say how much of either rise any president caused.

28
00:04:17,467 --> 00:04:23,554
Both answers turned on the calendar: which month
counts as now, and how long a stretch you measure.

29
00:04:23,567 --> 00:04:29,179
So when someone tells you prices are lower,
ask: compared with when, and over how long?

30
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:33,175
And when the pump price moves, check
what crude oil did that month.
